 The exploratory HRES comparison used 1,970 common samples valid at 06 and 18~UTC from 22 April 2019 to 31 December 2021, with matched 00/12~UTC origins and six-hour leads. Against the final-hour ERA5 target, HRES had RMSE 0.2061~mm\,h$^{-1}$, compared with 0.2428--0.2622~mm\,h$^{-1}$ for the six learning models; all six paired RMSE differences favoured HRES with statistical support. For ERA5 exceedance events at wet $\geq0.1$~mm\,h$^{-1}$, Q95 and $\geq1.0$~mm\,h$^{-1}$, HRES ROC AUC was 0.941, 0.959 and 0.969, respectively; at each threshold, paired comparisons against all six learning models supported higher AUC for HRES. Against Dublin Airport observations, HRES event AUC was significantly higher than that of all six models, whereas its RMSE advantage had statistical support only against XGBoost and Transformer. HRES represents the complete six-hour total converted to a mean hourly rate, while the learning models predict only the final hour. Airport observations represent a point rather than the regional mean, and ERA5 and HRES share the IFS modelling framework. These differences limit interpretation: this is an exploratory reference comparison, not a fully matched-target benchmark, and it does not establish NWP competitiveness or a computational-cost advantage for the models.
 \par}
 \textcolor{red}{\hypersetup{linkcolor=red}Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:hres_era5_rmse} and~\ref{supp-stab:hres_era5_auc} report the ERA5-target RMSE and AUC comparisons; Tables~\ref{supp-stab:hres_station_rmse} and~\ref{supp-stab:hres_station_auc} report the corresponding airport-target results. The accumulation-window and spatial-support differences stated above apply to all four tables.}
+
+\revminor{The retrieved HRES subset contains lead steps 0 and 6 h, not the intervening +5 h amount needed to recover a final-hour forecast; no strictly matched final-hour NWP comparison is available in these saved experiments. Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3nwp_common_context} adds the common-subset reference scores and the ERA5 six-hour-average diagnostic (RMSE 0.1882~mm\,h$^{-1}$ against the final-hour target). That diagnostic uses post-origin verifying information and is neither an available forecast nor a theoretical error bound. Dividing a six-hour accumulation by six changes its units but does not recover its final hour. The observed score contrasts therefore remain descriptive comparisons of products with different temporal support, rather than a fair ranking of operational forecasting skill.}
 
